## Key Findings

- Twinkling PMMA showed substantially greater temporal variability than metal.
- Wall filtering suppressed stationary signal components and revealed differences associated with the twinkling response.
- Beamformed RF spectra from PMMA showed broader and more irregular frequency content than metal.
- Spectral differences remained after normalization, indicating that the distinction was not caused only by differences in signal magnitude.
- IQ analysis preserved the stability-versus-variability contrast observed in the RF domain.
- Pulse-to-pulse variation and spectral variability provide quantitative features for distinguishing twinkling from a stable reflector.

## Significance

These results show that Doppler twinkling has measurable signatures before formation of the final color Doppler image.

The combination of temporal variability and spectral analysis provides a framework for studying how twinkling develops through the ultrasound processing chain. These features may also support the development of optimized ultrasound-visible markers and quantitative twinkling detection methods.

